        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Generac vs Kohler",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/generac-vs-kohler/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Kohler better than Generac?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Kohler is slightly better built in several details, including enclosure finish, motor starting and warranty wording, but not by enough to override service access. Generac has more than 9,400 dealers and far easier parts availability. Kohler is better when you have a strong local dealer; Generac is better almost everywhere else, and the price difference is usually modest."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which is quieter, Generac or Kohler?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On spec sheets, the Generac Guardian 22kW is rated at about 67 dB(A) at full load and 57 dB(A) in its five-minute Quiet-Test exercise. The Kohler 20RCA is rated 69 dB(A) at full speed and 64 dB(A) in its twenty-minute weekly exercise, both at 7 meters. Placement changes real-world noise more than brand does."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do Generac and Kohler use the same engine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No, but they are very similar. Generac uses its own G-Force 999cc overhead-valve V-twin on its larger Guardian units, and Kohler uses its Command PRO CH1000, also 999cc, on the 20RCA. Both run at 3,600 rpm and are designed for standby duty. Installers rate both as durable, with Kohler often given a small longevity edge."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do Generac and Kohler generators last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With annual maintenance, air-cooled units from either brand commonly last fifteen to twenty years or roughly 2,000-3,000 run hours, whichever comes first. Liquid-cooled models last longer because they run at 1,800 rpm. Neglected batteries, skipped oil changes and corroded cabinets in coastal areas shorten life far more than brand choice does."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a Kohler generator more expensive than a Generac?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, but not by much. A Kohler unit typically costs a few hundred to about a thousand dollars more than the equivalent Generac. Installed totals for a 20-22kW unit from either brand run roughly $9,000-13,000, and installer pricing varies more than the brand premium does, so compare complete quotes rather than unit prices."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Generac Mobile Link cost money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Basic Mobile Link monitoring is free on Wi-Fi-equipped Guardian units and shows status, exercise results and maintenance reminders. Real-time push, text and email alerts require a Premium subscription, which for a single generator costs a few dollars a month, with web billing cheaper than buying through the app. Check the current plan list before you rely on alerts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I switch my Kohler or Generac from natural gas to propane?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Both brands' air-cooled residential units are field-convertible between natural gas and propane, typically by a dealer during installation or later. Output changes with the fuel: propane gives the full rated figure, while natural gas runs roughly 10-15% lower. Propane runtime depends on tank size, so size the tank alongside the generator."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
