    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most homeowners, Generac is the safer buy, because the generator is only as good as the dealer who installs and services it, and Generac's network is many times larger. Kohler is the better buy when you have a strong local Kohler dealer, live near salt air, or care about a more thorough weekly self-test and a warranty that includes labor for the full five years.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generac and Kohler build their 20-26kW air-cooled home standby generators around strikingly similar hardware: a 999cc overhead-valve V-twin spinning at 3,600 rpm, a brushless alternator rated at under 5% total harmonic distortion, an aluminum enclosure, and a five-year base warranty. If you put the spec sheets side by side, the differences are small enough that the brand on the cabinet should not be the first thing you decide.
          </p>

          <p>
            What actually separates them is everything around the box. Generac reported more than 9,400 residential dealers in its most recent annual filing, and most industry estimates put its share of the US home standby market at around three quarters. Kohler, now sold under the Rehlko corporate name after Kohler Energy was spun out in 2024, has a much thinner residential dealer base and a much deeper commercial and industrial one. That gap matters more than any engine specification, because a standby generator is a fifteen-year service relationship.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest decision rule is local. Get a quote from your best-reviewed Generac dealer and your best-reviewed Kohler dealer. If both are solid, the Kohler gives you a more thorough weekly self-test, a cleaner warranty, and a reputation for longevity in coastal air, typically for a few hundred dollars to roughly a thousand more installed. If the nearest Kohler dealer is ninety minutes away or slow to return calls, buy the Generac and do not look back.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have not settled on a size yet, start there instead, because the wrong size costs more than the wrong brand. Our ranked picks, including the Generac Guardian 22kW and the Kohler 20RCA head to head, are in <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">our guide to the best whole house generators</a>. And if you are still deciding whether you need a permanent unit at all, read <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby generator</a> first; a well-chosen portable with an interlock covers a lot of homes for a third of the money.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Choose the dealer first, the brand second.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Engines, alternators and enclosures are close enough that installation quality and service response will decide whether your generator starts in an ice storm. A great Kohler dealer beats an average Generac dealer, and vice versa.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lineups" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Current Lineups, Model by Model
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generac's residential air-cooled line is the Guardian series, currently offered at 10, 14, 18, 22, 24 and 26kW. Ratings are quoted on propane, with natural gas lower: the Guardian 22kW makes 22kW on LP and 19.5kW on natural gas, the 24kW drops to roughly 21kW on gas, and the 26kW to roughly 22.5kW. Above that, Generac's liquid-cooled Protector series runs at 1,800 rpm for larger and all-electric homes. Guardian units are widely sold as packages with a 200-amp service-entrance Smart Switch and Wi-Fi monitoring built in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kohler's current residential air-cooled line is the RCA family: the 14RCA, 20RCA and 26RCA, with an L suffix (20RCAL, 26RCAL) indicating a package with a matched automatic transfer switch. The 20RCA is rated 20kW on LP and 18kW on natural gas. If you see quotes or listings for the 20RESC or 20RESCL, those are the older generation that the RCA line replaced; they share the same engine family but older controls and enclosure. Above the RCA line, Kohler offers liquid-cooled residential units at 24, 30, 38, 48 and 60kW, such as the 24RCLA and 30RCLA, which run at 1,800 rpm.
          </p>

          <p>
            The size steps are where the two brands genuinely differ. Generac offers more rungs on the ladder in the air-cooled range, which makes it easier to land on exactly the size you need, especially at the 18kW point that suits many 2,000 square foot homes with one AC system. Kohler jumps from 14 to 20 to 26kW, but its liquid-cooled range starts at 24kW, lower than many people expect, which makes a quiet, slow-turning engine reachable for a mid-size house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural gas derating is similar on both and is the number to size from if you are on utility gas. Every rating on the box is the propane figure, and you will typically lose 10-15% on natural gas. If you are choosing between fuel types or want our picks for gas-fed homes specifically, see <a href="/best-natural-gas-generator/">the best natural gas generators</a>, which covers meter capacity and line sizing as well as the units themselves.
          </p>

