        <div id="engines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Engines and Power Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generac builds its own engines, and the Guardian 22kW, 24kW and 26kW use the G-Force 999cc V-twin, an overhead-valve industrial design with pressure lubrication that Generac developed specifically for standby duty rather than adapting from lawn equipment. Kohler's 20RCA uses the Kohler Command PRO CH1000, also a 999cc overhead-valve V-twin at 3,600 rpm, from an engine family with a long commercial track record in mowers, light industrial equipment and generators. Kohler makes a point of the commercial lineage, and installers who service both tend to rate the Command PRO slightly better for longevity, though neither engine has a reputation problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because both engines are air-cooled and run at 3,600 rpm under load, they share the same weakness: heat and wear on long continuous runs. Neither is designed to run for weeks the way a liquid-cooled 1,800 rpm unit can. Both manufacturers recommend service at roughly 100-200 run hours, which a single long hurricane outage can reach. If you live somewhere with multi-day outages every year, that is an argument for stepping up to a liquid-cooled model from either brand rather than choosing between these two engines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power quality is effectively a tie. Generac markets its alternator as True Power, rated at under 5% total harmonic distortion, and Kohler rates the RCA line at under 5% THD from no load to full load. Under 5% is the threshold that matters for modern electronics, variable-speed HVAC blowers, and inverter-driven appliances, and both clear it. Any claim that one brand is meaningfully cleaner for your television or furnace board is marketing rather than measurement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Motor starting is where Kohler has a modest, real edge. Kohler's PowerBoost system is designed to hold voltage up while a large motor such as a central air compressor or a well pump starts, and installers who work on both frequently report less light flicker and fewer nuisance drops when a big compressor kicks on under existing load. Generac handles this with its Smart Management Modules, which shed a load briefly rather than trying to start everything at once. Both approaches work; Kohler's simply needs less help on the same size unit.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Spec Sheet Snapshot, 20-22kW Class</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">999 cc</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Both engines, OHV V-twin</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3,600</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rpm under load, both</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">&lt;5%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rated THD, both</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">18-19.5 kW</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Natural gas output, Kohler vs Generac</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Noise and the Weekly Exercise Cycle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every standby generator starts itself on a schedule to keep the engine lubricated, the battery charged, and to flush out problems before an outage does. This exercise cycle is the noise your neighbors will hear most often, far more than the generator running during an actual outage, so it deserves more attention than the full-load number on the spec sheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generac's Guardian units run a five-minute exercise that you can set weekly, every other week, or monthly. In Quiet-Test mode the engine runs at reduced speed, and Generac rates it at 57 dB(A) at 23 feet, about the level of a normal conversation. At full load the Guardian 22kW is rated around 67 dB(A) at the same distance. Kohler's 20RCA runs a 20-minute weekly exercise with complete system diagnostics, rated at 64 dB(A) at 7 meters, and 69 dB(A) at full speed. Kohler also offers an unloaded full-speed exercise and, with its RXT transfer switch, a loaded exercise that actually transfers the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            On paper, then, the Generac is the quieter neighbor during the weekly test, both in volume and duration: five minutes at 57 dB versus twenty minutes at 64 dB. The trade is thoroughness. Kohler's longer cycle and diagnostics are designed to catch slow-developing problems, and installers note that a generator that only ever runs for five minutes at low speed can hide issues that appear under load. Neither approach is wrong, but you should know which one you are buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Real-world sound depends heavily on placement. A unit tucked into an inside corner between two walls will be noticeably louder than the rated figure, and one facing a neighbor's bedroom window will generate complaints regardless of brand. Kohler states the RCA line can be installed as close as 18 inches to a structure, subject to local code; both brands also require several feet of clearance from windows, doors and air intakes for exhaust reasons. Placement and pad work are part of the quote, and <a href="/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/">our whole house generator installation cost breakdown</a> shows how much siting decisions add.
          </p>

          <p>
            If noise is your top priority, the honest answer is neither air-cooled line. Kohler's liquid-cooled 24RCLA is rated at 61 dB(A) at full speed and 54 dB(A) during exercise because it turns at 1,800 rpm, and Generac's liquid-cooled Protector units are similarly quieter than their air-cooled siblings. You pay substantially more for the privilege, but you get a machine designed for long continuous runs as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-dealers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Warranty Terms and the Dealer Network
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands start at five years, but the wording is not the same. Kohler states its residential warranty as five years or 2,000 hours, covering parts, labor and travel, for on-grid installations. Generac calls the Guardian coverage a five-year limited warranty, and several authorized dealers describe it as parts, labor and limited travel in the first two years, with later years focused on major components. Read the actual warranty statement for the exact model you are quoted, because terms change and the difference between labor included and labor excluded in year four can be a thousand-dollar repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands sell extended coverage. Generac offers seven- and ten-year extensions on Guardian units that must be purchased within twelve months of the generator, with the ten-year plan commonly listed around a thousand dollars and capped at 2,000 hours. Kohler sells extensions too and has repeatedly run promotions that include a ten-year extended warranty free with qualifying models, including the 14RCA, 20RCA and 26RCA. If you are buying Kohler, ask whether a promotion is running before you sign; it is a real thousand-dollar difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            The dealer network is the single biggest practical difference between these brands. With more than 9,400 dealers, Generac has someone authorized in almost every county, parts on regional shelves, and techs who have seen every common fault many times. After a hurricane or ice storm, when every service company has a waiting list, that depth translates into faster repairs. Kohler's residential network is smaller and uneven; it is strong in some regions and thin in others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before choosing Kohler, call the nearest two authorized Kohler dealers and ask three questions: how many residential units they installed last year, whether they stock common parts, and what their typical response time was after the last major storm. If the answers are good, you lose little by choosing Kohler. If the answers are vague, the theoretical advantages of the engine or warranty will not help you while you wait two weeks for a technician.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution that applies to both: warranty claims depend on documented maintenance and proper registration. Keep every service record, register the unit the week it is installed, and make sure the installer files whatever start-up paperwork the manufacturer requires. Most denied claims trace back to missing records, not to the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hardware" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Enclosures, Transfer Switches and Smart Monitoring
          </h2>

          <p>
            Current Guardian 22-26kW units use an aluminum enclosure with Generac's RhinoCoat powder finish, a real improvement over the steel cabinets on older Generacs that rusted in humid climates. Kohler's RCA enclosure is aluminum with an automotive-grade textured finish, and Kohler's reputation for holding up near salt water is well earned among coastal installers. If you live within a few miles of the ocean, the Kohler cabinet is the safer long-term bet, though a modern Generac is no longer the liability older units were.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transfer switches are where a lot of the installed cost hides. Generac packages most Guardians with a 200-amp service-entrance Smart Switch and pairs it with Smart Management Modules that briefly shed large loads such as an AC compressor or a water heater when demand spikes. Kohler pairs the RCA line with its Model RXT automatic transfer switch, which includes a combined interface and load management board. Both systems are competent, and both let a smaller generator cover more of the house. For how switch type, amperage and service-entrance rating drive the price, see <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">our guide to transfer switch types, cost and installation</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Monitoring is closer than the marketing suggests. Generac's Mobile Link is built into Guardian units with Wi-Fi and offers a free Basic tier showing status, exercise results and maintenance reminders. Real-time push, text and email alerts sit behind a Premium subscription, which since May 2025 has been priced at a few dollars a month for a single generator, with web billing cheaper than in-app billing. Read the plan list before you assume an outage alert will reach your phone for free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kohler's long-running OnCue Plus system works with its RDC2 and DC2 controllers and shows real-time power status, the transfer switch, and optional load control modules. Kohler has been moving owners to a newer Kohler Energy Management app, now published under the Rehlko name, with continuous monitoring and maintenance records. Confirm with your dealer which app your controller supports and what, if anything, it costs, because the transition means older listings and forum advice are often out of date.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, whichever app you get, the feature that matters is an alert when the exercise fails or the unit throws a fault. Set that up on day one. A standby generator that has quietly been in fault mode for three months is the most common way people discover, mid-outage, that their expensive insurance policy is not working.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Installed Price and Who Each Brand Suits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installed, a 20-22kW air-cooled unit from either brand typically lands between $9,000 and $13,000, with 10-14kW units around $6,500-9,000. The generator itself is a bit under half of that; the rest is the transfer switch, electrical work, gas line, pad, permits, and in many cases a gas meter upgrade that adds $1,500-3,000. Kohler's unit price usually runs a few hundred to about a thousand dollars above the equivalent Generac, and that gap is often smaller than the difference between two installers quoting the same brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generac's price advantage is partly scale and partly competition: with so many dealers, you can usually get three Generac quotes, which keeps margins honest. For a detailed look at what Guardian units cost by size, and which line items inflate quotes, see <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">what a Generac generator really costs</a>. The same line items apply to Kohler, so that breakdown is a useful checklist whichever brand you choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Generac if you want the widest choice of sizes, the most installers to quote against, the easiest parts and service after a regional storm, and a quieter five-minute weekly test. It is the right default for suburban homes on natural gas, buyers who want the 18kW size, and anyone outside a strong Kohler dealer's territory. The Guardian 22kW is our overall pick in <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">the best whole house generator rankings</a> for exactly those reasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Kohler if you have a reputable, responsive Kohler dealer nearby, live in a coastal or humid area, run large motor loads such as a well pump or a big central air compressor, or value a warranty that states labor and travel for the full five years. It is also worth a hard look if a free ten-year warranty promotion is running. The 20RCA is a better-built machine in small ways that add up over fifteen years, provided someone local can keep it running.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Generac by default, Kohler by dealer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Generac wins on network, sizing choice and price competition. Kohler wins on build details, motor starting and warranty clarity. Let the quality of your local installers break the tie.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the brand before checking the dealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The best-built generator is a poor buy if the nearest authorized service is two hours away. Call the local dealers for both brands, ask about storm response times and parts stock, and let those answers carry real weight. Service access decides more outcomes than engine design does over fifteen years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing propane ratings when you burn natural gas</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both brands quote the propane figure on the box. On natural gas, the Guardian 22kW makes 19.5kW and the Kohler 20RCA makes 18kW. Size from the natural gas number if that is your fuel, or you may end up a size short when the air conditioning and well pump start together.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the warranty includes labor for five years</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kohler states parts, labor and travel for five years or 2,000 hours. Generac's five-year limited warranty is described by many dealers as front-loaded, with labor coverage narrowing after year two. Read the actual statement for your model, and decide on an extension within the twelve-month purchase window.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the exercise cycle when siting the unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Neighbors hear the weekly test far more than outage running. A Kohler 20RCA runs twenty minutes weekly at 64 dB(A); a Guardian runs five minutes at 57 dB(A) in Quiet-Test. Avoid inside corners and spots facing bedroom windows, and choose an exercise schedule that suits the household.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never setting up fault alerts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both Mobile Link and Kohler's app can warn you when an exercise fails, but only if the unit is connected and alerts are switched on. On Generac, real-time alerts need a Premium plan. Many owners discover a months-old fault during the outage the generator was bought for. Set alerts on installation day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Kohler better than Generac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kohler is slightly better built in several details, including enclosure finish, motor starting and warranty wording, but not by enough to override service access. Generac has more than 9,400 dealers and far easier parts availability. Kohler is better when you have a strong local dealer; Generac is better almost everywhere else, and the price difference is usually modest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is quieter, Generac or Kohler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On spec sheets, the Generac Guardian 22kW is rated at about 67 dB(A) at full load and 57 dB(A) in its five-minute Quiet-Test exercise. The Kohler 20RCA is rated 69 dB(A) at full speed and 64 dB(A) in its twenty-minute weekly exercise, both at 7 meters. Placement changes real-world noise more than brand does.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Generac and Kohler use the same engine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, but they are very similar. Generac uses its own G-Force 999cc overhead-valve V-twin on its larger Guardian units, and Kohler uses its Command PRO CH1000, also 999cc, on the 20RCA. Both run at 3,600 rpm and are designed for standby duty. Installers rate both as durable, with Kohler often given a small longevity edge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do Generac and Kohler generators last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With annual maintenance, air-cooled units from either brand commonly last fifteen to twenty years or roughly 2,000-3,000 run hours, whichever comes first. Liquid-cooled models last longer because they run at 1,800 rpm. Neglected batteries, skipped oil changes and corroded cabinets in coastal areas shorten life far more than brand choice does.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Kohler generator more expensive than a Generac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, but not by much. A Kohler unit typically costs a few hundred to about a thousand dollars more than the equivalent Generac. Installed totals for a 20-22kW unit from either brand run roughly $9,000-13,000, and installer pricing varies more than the brand premium does, so compare complete quotes rather than unit prices.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Generac Mobile Link cost money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Basic Mobile Link monitoring is free on Wi-Fi-equipped Guardian units and shows status, exercise results and maintenance reminders. Real-time push, text and email alerts require a Premium subscription, which for a single generator costs a few dollars a month, with web billing cheaper than buying through the app. Check the current plan list before you rely on alerts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I switch my Kohler or Generac from natural gas to propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Both brands' air-cooled residential units are field-convertible between natural gas and propane, typically by a dealer during installation or later. Output changes with the fuel: propane gives the full rated figure, while natural gas runs roughly 10-15% lower. Propane runtime depends on tank size, so size the tank alongside the generator.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

